      "role": "user",
      "content": "Definition: Understandability refers to how easily developers can comprehend and maintain the test code.\n\nTask: Improve the understandability of the test `ByteOrderMarkTest_testConstantCharsetNames.java` (path:- /Users/tenghaha/Projects/New_Agent_understandability_improvement/data/original/commons-io/manual/testcases/ByteOrderMarkTest/ByteOrderMarkTest_testConstantCharsetNames.java). The refactored code must be syntactically correct and compilable against the provided CUT signatures. Do not alter the runtime behaviour of the original test: the same methods must be called with the same arguments and produce the same expected outcomes.\n"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I'll read both the original test and the CUT source to understand what needs to be improved.Now let me check the compile.sh to understand the classpath setup.Now I have a clear picture. The original test has several understandability issues:\n1. Unused static fields `TEST_BOM_1/2/3` that are irrelevant to this test method\n2. Unused imports (assertArrayEquals, assertFalse, assertNotEquals, assertThrows, assertTrue)\n3. Assertions lack failure messages — a reader can't tell which BOM encoding failed\n4. No explanation of *why* `assertNotNull` proves the charset name is valid (loading via `Charset.forName` either returns a Charset or throws, so non-null is the proof)\n\nI'll produce a cleaned-up version: remove noise, trim imports, and add descriptive assertion messages.`COMPILE_OK` — the improved test compiles cleanly. The refactored version removes the three unused `TEST_BOM_*` fields and all unused imports, expands the Javadoc to explain the `assertNotNull`/`Charset.forName` contract, and adds a per-encoding failure message to each assertion so a future reader (or a failing test output) immediately knows which BOM constant's charset name is unrecognised."
    }
  ],
  "last_code": "package org.apache.commons.io;\n\nimport static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertNotNull;\n\nimport java.nio.charset.Charset;\n\nimport org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;\n\npublic class ByteOrderMarkTest_testConstantCharsetNames {\n\n    /**\n     * Verifies that every predefined {@link ByteOrderMark} constant carries a charset name\n     * that is recognised by the JVM. {@link Charset#forName(String)} returns a non-null\n     * {@link Charset} on success and throws {@link java.nio.charset.UnsupportedCharsetException}\n     * on failure, so a non-null result proves the name is valid.\n     */\n    @Test\n    void testConstantCharsetNames() {\n        assertNotNull(Charset.forName(ByteOrderMark.UTF_8.getCharsetName()),\n                \"UTF-8 charset name reported by ByteOrderMark.UTF_8 must be JVM-recognised\");\n        assertNotNull(Charset.forName(ByteOrderMark.UTF_16BE.getCharsetName()),\n                \"UTF-16BE charset name reported by ByteOrderMark.UTF_16BE must be JVM-recognised\");\n        assertNotNull(Charset.forName(ByteOrderMark.UTF_16LE.getCharsetName()),\n                \"UTF-16LE charset name reported by ByteOrderMark.UTF_16LE must be JVM-recognised\");\n        assertNotNull(Charset.forName(ByteOrderMark.UTF_32BE.getCharsetName()),\n                \"UTF-32BE charset name reported by ByteOrderMark.UTF_32BE must be JVM-recognised\");\n        assertNotNull(Charset.forName(ByteOrderMark.UTF_32LE.getCharsetName()),\n                \"UTF-32LE charset name reported by ByteOrderMark.UTF_32LE must be JVM-recognised\");\n    }\n}\n",
